      "value": "@{5ca74f3fb98a820b3ffec9cf|full_name}: If it's that easy then that's something to try - but why should there be any other mode? What is the actual use case for the camera being in the wrong place? Who asked for that? I definitely did not.\n\nAnd what would even be the point of _having_ the view dot, if it isn't going to actually be used? We spend time and effort putting it in the correct position, only for VRC to actively ignore the setting we spent time getting right.\n\nIn my eyes, if an app has settings which could be called something like \"behave as people would expect\" - then those settings should not exist, and the app should just behave correctly in the first place. Doing anything else is bad UX.\n\nAnd while I'm at it: In general, adding more settings is also bad UX. It also shows that your designer doesn't know the right way for things to behave. If you have a good designer, they figure out the correct way for things to behave, and they tell the development team to make it work in the one way which is correct. If you have a bad designer, or just no designer, you end up with settings screens with 100 checkboxes and sliders and nobody knows what the correct setting is for any given thing. Guess which end of this spectrum VRChat is currently near?\n\nAnd just in case you want to pull the argument \"but some users want it the other way so it has to be an option\" - that's cool, but to get that one by me, you would have to find me a user who wants the camera to be in the wrong place, and that user has to give me a cohesive, convincing argument to convince me that I might want the camera in the wrong place. Good luck finding such a person. And even if such a person did exist: (1) it's still obviously the wrong default, because such a person is exceedingly rare, and (2) if such an important option had to be enabled in order to get an acceptable experience, it should be mentioned during the onboarding process - but the default is still incorrect. Certainly, discovering that an option to do the right thing exists only after getting angry on a ticket, is not the ideal outcome for VRChat or for any of its users.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    },
